# Reducing scan overhead without reusing stale evidence

The first ordinary GEMM preflight completed 66 real-weight contracts covering all 15 ordinary families at 4K. The complete requested domain remains 10,376 contracts. Its journal at `outputs/gemm-deployment-v30` is preserved as evidence from the original controller and binary; the longscan controller does not import those rows into a new run.

Two repeated CPU costs can be reduced without changing the timed GEMM operation: decoding the same checkpoint block for every shape and rewriting the growing JSON report after every case.

## Decode once, give every measurement its own tensors

`tuning/gemm_deployment_longscan.py.draft` wraps one `WeightArchive` instance in a bounded CPU cache. `WeightArchive` reads the stage files into immutable Python byte buffers and verifies their SHA-256 values at construction. Its decoder has one argument, the block number; decoded precision and layout are fixed by that implementation. Each cache belongs to that one archive and is discarded with the process.

The cache keeps private contiguous CPU tensor copies. Every lookup returns new clones in a read-only mapping, so a measurement cannot change the cached tensors by modifying its returned weights. Master tensors are created outside inference mode so their mutation versions can be checked. Their version, shape, dtype and address are checked before reuse. The cache also owns its masters independently of the decoder's returned tensors.

The default resident-cache budget is 1GiB. Least recently used blocks are evicted when necessary; a block larger than the budget is decoded and returned without caching. This budget covers resident master tensors. Temporary decoder arrays, returned clones and GPU tensors are outside it. The existing measurement still creates and packs its own GPU operands and checks their immutability.

A bounded CPU-only block31 experiment measured three ordinary decodes and three warm cache lookups. Median decode time was 529.83ms; median warm lookup, including cloned output tensors, was 1.67ms. The first cache fill took 546.55ms. The underlying report is `outputs/gemm_deployment_longscan_cpu_timing.json.draft`. These are CPU decode measurements, not end-to-end scan timings or GPU speedups.

## Keep SQLite durable and export JSON periodically

Every validated case still commits to SQLite immediately. JSON becomes a periodic readable snapshot: by default after 32 newly committed cases or 60 elapsed seconds, whichever is reached first at a case boundary. Clean completion, failure, interruption through Python, and a completed no-op resume all force a final export.

JSON is serialized to a temporary file in the same directory, flushed, and replaced atomically. A hard process termination may leave the JSON behind the latest SQLite commits. Resume reads SQLite and skips already committed contracts; it does not trust the JSON's progress count as the durable authority.

Full source, runtime, extension-binary and checkpoint-manifest identity checks remain before and after every measurement, and before every JSON export. No binary hash or driver query is cached. This removes the former third identity query from most cases only because those cases no longer perform a JSON export. It does not shorten the before/after measurement protection. A long individual measurement can exceed the JSON time interval; checks and export occur when that case ends.

The report separates identity-check time, measurement wall time, verification time, SQLite commit time and prior JSON export time. Measurement wall time includes CPU preparation, correctness checks and GPU waits. It is not the CUDA event latency used to select a kernel. Cache hit/miss, copy time, decode time and resident-byte counters are recorded separately. Current-report serialization time cannot be included in that same completed report, so its scope explicitly says it includes earlier exports only.

## New controller, new journal

The resume digest includes a new controller protocol, cache budget and export intervals in addition to the existing full manifest, measurement settings, candidate harness, sources, policies, driver/libraries, GPU UUID, extension hash and checkpoint manifest. It rejects the original controller's journal even if dimensions and timing settings match. Changing the candidate catalog or compiling another private experiment likewise requires a fresh journal; no earlier rows acquire the new identity retroactively.

After review and activation, a bounded preflight would use a fresh output directory:

```powershell
python -m tuning.gemm_deployment_longscan --execute --max-cases 66 --output-dir outputs/gemm-deployment-final-catalog
```

The final kernel catalog should be settled before launching the full range. Once its binary, source and settings are pinned, continue that same directory without `--max-cases`. A changed budget or interval also needs a new directory because it is part of the controller identity.

All measurement correctness checks, paired candidate timing, common-winner requirements, complete-family export gates, rejected-shape guard anchors and C++ nearest/clamp rules come from the existing scanner unchanged. The longscan controller has not been GPU-tested or activated. Its CPU tests cover cache ownership and eviction, inference-mode access, real block31 tensor-byte parity, immediate commits with periodic snapshots, failure/resume, final-case and no-op identity checks, rejection of the old journal, and atomic replacement failure. Native-DLL speed and the 85% throughput target remain separate gates.
